      "role": "user",
      "content": "Those terms—\"Record Tampering\" and \"Information Manipulation\"—are helpful for the technical side. But I'm still struggling with how to handle the *nature* of these disruptions in the provenance metadata. \n\nIn some of these 19th-century field notes, the descriptions of these \"disruptive agents\" go beyond mere data errors; they are described as small, highly organized, and almost predatory in how they interact with physical objects and records—often hiding in small gaps or shadows and leaving behind messes that feel more like intentional sabotage than random accidents. \n\nEven if we use \"Information Manipulation,\" we still have a gap in describing the *type* of entity causing the chaos. Is there a way to categorize these \"trickster\" style entities using established archival or anthropological frameworks (like those found in RDA) that acknowledges their tendency for small-scale, disruptive, or even \"nuisance\" behaviors without crossing the line into being unscientific or purely mythological?",
      "source": "evaluator"
